diff --git a/poteto-mode/references/pi-tools.md b/poteto-mode/references/pi-tools.md
index 153e4cb..f2e59a8 100644
--- a/poteto-mode/references/pi-tools.md
+++ b/poteto-mode/references/pi-tools.md
@@ -45,7 +45,7 @@ poteto-mode's Subagents section applies on Pi through the `agent` tool:
 - Agents belong to the session that started them. Quitting, reloading, and starting, resuming, or forking a session all stop every running agent, and a parent process that exits signals its agents to stop. On restore, an agent still running under its launching Pi process is left alone. An orphan whose process identity matches the saved record is signalled to stop, and remains unavailable to `send_message` and `stop_agent` until its process exits. Without enough identity data to signal it safely, its process is left alone and continuation is refused until its exit is confirmed. Only then is the restored agent marked `stopped` and allowed to resume.
 - Each child's environment names the session that started it in `PI_SUBAGENT_PARENT_SESSION`, the variable `@gotgenes/pi-permission-system` reads to forward a child's permission ask to that session. Install that package globally so children load it too. As of its 42.0.0, the starting session then shows the ask at its own prompt while it has a UI, which `pi -p` does not. The ask names the child's `subagent_type`, which the package reads from an `<active_agent>` tag the extension adds to each child's system prompt. The package also applies the `permission:` frontmatter of an agent file with that name in Pi's `agents` folder or the project's `.pi/agents`; it does not read `pstack-agents`. A permission dialog the child opens itself reaches the pstack extension, which cancels every child dialog, so that call is denied as if the user had refused.
 - `send_message` to a running agent is an RPC `steer` on the child's stdin. The agent reads it after its current tool calls, as on Claude Code, carries on in the same run, and sends one completion notice. A message the child rejects returns an error, and the agent keeps running. A message to a finished agent resumes its session in the background with the context of its earlier runs. A message that arrives after the agent settled but before its process exited resumes the agent once the process has exited.
-- A role value's `@<level>`, or the sheet's `default effort` level, is passed as the `agent` tool's `effort`, which the extension passes to the child as `--thinking`. `session` omits it, so the child runs at its agent file's `effort`, else at the parent's current thinking level.
+- A role value's `@<level>`, or else the level poteto-mode's [Reasoning effort](../SKILL.md#reasoning-effort) gives it (`medium` for a value that resolves to an Opus model, the sheet's `default effort` level otherwise), is passed as the `agent` tool's `effort`, which the extension passes to the child as `--thinking`. `session` omits it, so the child runs at its agent file's `effort`, else at the parent's current thinking level.
 - An agent file's `model:` and `effort:` frontmatter are defaults for a call that omits them, as on Claude Code, so the `agent` call's `model` and `effort` win. Claude Code's `model: inherit` runs the agent on the parent's model. The plugin's agent files set neither.
 - Keep the rest of the policy unchanged. Pass file pointers not inlined context, give each worker its own worktree when they write, review every subagent's diff yourself.
 
